    "1.1.0": "MAJOR CRYPTOGRAPHIC ENHANCEMENT: Extended hash algorithm support with complete SHA-2 family (SHA-224/384) and SHA-3 family (SHA3-224/384), added BLAKE3 ultra-fast hashing and SHAKE-128 extendable-output function. Implemented RFC 5869 HKDF key derivation with configurable hash algorithms and iteration rounds. Added comprehensive post-quantum signature support with MAYO-1/3/5 multivariate and CROSS-128/192/256 code-based algorithms. Complete GUI redesign with professional hash family organization (SHA-2, SHA-3, BLAKE, SHAKE, Legacy) and dedicated KDF Algorithm Settings section. Enhanced CLI with full coverage of all hash algorithms and HKDF configuration options. Improved security posture by reclassifying PBKDF2 as legacy KDF (disabled by default). Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. Extensive testing infrastructure with 93+ CLI parameter validation, dual implementation consistency testing, removal of deprecated pqc-allow-mixed-operations parameter, and systematic cleanup of 63 development artifacts. Comprehensive documentation updates reflecting modern vs. legacy algorithm categorization.",
    "1.2.0": "PROFESSIONAL FLUTTER DESKTOP & SECURITY HARDENING: Complete migration from tkinter to Flutter desktop providing native Wayland and X11 support, advanced CLI integration with real-time progress monitoring, comprehensive settings system with theme switching and cryptographic defaults. Desktop UX excellence with menu bar, keyboard shortcuts, drag & drop file operations, NavigationRail sidebar, tabbed interface, and algorithm recommendation engine with security guidance. Added advanced algorithm configuration UI for all KDFs (Argon2, Scrypt, Balloon, HKDF) and post-quantum algorithms (ML-KEM, Kyber, HQC, MAYO, CROSS). Enhanced security through cryptographic hardening: permanently removed deprecated PBKDF2 key derivation and Whirlpool hash algorithms from new encryption operations (existing encrypted files with these algorithms can still be decrypted). Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. Streamlined Flatpak architecture removing complex X11/XWayland compatibility layers. Professional desktop integration with native dialogs and comprehensive JSON schema validation system for keystore and metadata structures.",
    "1.2.1": "Documentation maintenance release: Minor updates to documentation, user guides, and help text to improve clarity and user experience. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development. No functional changes to cryptographic operations or core features.",
    "1.3.0": "CRYPTOGRAPHIC ENHANCEMENT & BUG FIXES: Added RandomX proof-of-work key derivation function optimized for CPUs with light (256MB) and fast (2GB) modes. Fixed critical default configuration decryption failure caused by metadata generation inconsistency between PBKDF2 parameter defaults and security template settings. Resolved post-quantum cryptography dual encryption test failures through improved test data parsing with binary prefix handling. Enhanced Docker build optimization reducing image size to 140MB with proper runtime library dependencies. Implemented comprehensive debug logging system replacing print statements. Added security enhancements: automatic application of default hash/KDF configuration when none specified to prevent direct password encryption, and security warnings when KDFs would operate directly on user password without sufficient entropy from prior hashing. Maintained full backward compatibility for all existing encrypted files while improving new encryption consistency. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development.",
    "1.3.1": "SECURITY & MAINTENANCE: Critical HSM security fix removing --device=all from Flatpak manifest, requiring explicit USB device opt-in for hardware security modules. Fixed post-quantum private key storage for AEAD algorithms. Enhanced Flatpak permissions with explicit Yubikey/smart card device access. Cleaned up Flatpak manifest removing invalid filesystem permissions.",
    "1.3.2": "SECURITY & PERFORMANCE MONITORING: Implemented comprehensive decryption time/memory estimation system to prevent DoS attacks via inflated metadata parameters. Added static benchmark data for all hash algorithms (SHA-224/256/384/512, SHA3-224/256/384/512, BLAKE2b/3, SHAKE-128/256) and KDF operations (PBKDF2, Argon2, Scrypt, Balloon, HKDF, RandomX). Pre-decryption cost display shows operation breakdown with conservative estimates, warns when thresholds exceeded (>10 seconds or >1GB memory), and provides 2-second cancellation window. Added --no-estimate CLI flag to suppress display for trusted files. Fixed critical argument parsing bug where --no-estimate failed when used with --progress flag due to preprocessor moving global flags before subcommand detection. Enhanced CLI robustness with improved subcommand detection after global flag preprocessing. Benchmarking system uses empirically validated non-linear scaling for memory-hard KDFs (Balloon: space_cost^0.85, Scrypt: exponential doubling). All existing encrypted files maintain full backward compatibility. Now includes enhanced Flutter GUI with configuration profiles, security improvements, and input validation backported from latest development.",
    "1.3.3": "LICENSE COMPLIANCE: Updated license declaration from MIT to Hippocratic License 3.0 across all distribution channels (PyPI, Flatpak). Fixed Flatpak metainfo.xml project_license field and added explicit license field to setup.py. No functional changes to cryptographic operations or features. Repository-wide license standardization for proper compliance.",
    "1.3.4": "CRITICAL SECURITY FIX (CVSSv3 8.1 HIGH): Fixed predictable salt derivation vulnerability (CWE-330) in multi-round KDF operations. Implemented Format Version 7 with secure chained salt derivation where each round uses previous round output as salt, preventing precomputation attacks. Affects all multi-round operations: BLAKE2b, BLAKE3, SHAKE-256, Argon2, Scrypt, Balloon, PBKDF2, HKDF. Full backward compatibility for decrypting Format Versions 3-6. Files encrypted with v1.3.4+ require v1.3.4 or newer to decrypt.",
    "1.3.5": "BLAKE3 HASH INTEGRATION: Added BLAKE3 keyed hashing support with 32-byte keys for format_version 7. Implemented BLAKE3-aware buffer sizing (64 bytes minimum) for proper key derivation with zero-initialization for deterministic hashing. Enhanced BLAKE3 detection in both flat (v3) and nested (v4+) hash config formats. Added comprehensive debug logging for BLAKE3 operations. Included build scripts for liboqs dependencies and Flatpak CI jobs. Fully backward compatible with v1.3.0-1.3.4 encrypted files and cross-version compatible with v1.4.x.",
    "1.4.0": "POST-QUANTUM ASYMMETRIC ENCRYPTION: Major release introducing Format Version 7 with post-quantum asymmetric encryption capabilities. Added ML-KEM-768 (Kyber) for key encapsulation and ML-DSA-65 (Dilithium) for digital signatures, providing quantum-resistant security. Implemented comprehensive Identity Management System with dual keypairs (encryption + signing), private key encryption at rest (Argon2id + AES-256-GCM), and identity storage in ~/.openssl_encrypt/identities/. Added multiple recipients support allowing single file encryption for N recipients with minimal overhead (~1.2KB per recipient). Implemented DoS Protection via signature verification before expensive KDF operations (fast ~1-5ms signature check prevents KDF attacks). Added complete Identity CLI with 7 commands (create, list, show, export, import, delete, change-password) for identity lifecycle management. Enhanced asymmetric encryption CLI with --for (multiple recipients), --sign-with (sender identity), --key (recipient for decryption), --verify-from (signature verification), and --no-verify flags. Integrated HSM Plugin Support with YubiKey Challenge-Response and plugin system architecture. Added benchmarking system for performance analysis and decryption time estimator for progress feedback. Comprehensive security enhancements: secure memory management (CryptoKey class, SecureBytes, secure_memzero), metadata integrity via digital signatures over canonical metadata, defense in depth with KDF chain running even in asymmetric mode. New modules: pqc_signing.py (450 lines), identity.py (750 lines), asymmetric_core.py (650 lines), identity_cli.py (540 lines). Extended modules: crypt_core.py (+700 lines), pqc.py (+100 lines). Added 120 new tests covering all asymmetric features with zero warnings. Format V7 maintains full backward compatibility with V6 symmetric encryption. No new dependencies (uses existing cryptography, argon2-cffi, liboqs-python). Production-ready with comprehensive documentation (ASYMMETRIC_ENCRYPTION_GUIDE.md), full test coverage, and security audit.",
    "1.4.0b8": "CRITICAL SECURITY FIX (BETA): Implemented Format Version 9 with secure chained salt derivation, resolving predictable salt issue in v1.4.x branch. Unified security model where v7 and v9 use secure chained derivation (unpredictable), v8 uses predictable derivation (backward compatibility only). Fixed critical pepper plugin scoping errors causing 100+ test failures. Added comprehensive Flatpak CI/CD pipeline with build and publish stages. Enhanced Flutter GUI with remote pepper plugin integration, integrity verification, cascade encryption UI, asymmetric encryption configuration, and YubiKey touch prompt improvements. Fixed HSM plugin loading and dependency management. Beta release addressing security vulnerability discovered in earlier v1.4.0 betas.",
    "1.4.0b9": "STABILITY & COMPATIBILITY (BETA): Test infrastructure improvements and cross-version compatibility fixes. Fixed 6 salt derivation test failures by correcting test suite to use generate_key() instead of multi_hash_password() for KDF operations. Completed Threefish-512/1024 cipher support with HKDF key expansion and proper AEAD integration. Fixed BLAKE3 buffer sizing for backward compatibility with pre-BLAKE3 encrypted files. Fixed critical metadata v7 schema compatibility with v1.3.4 (made 'mode' field optional). Resolved Scrypt bytearray conversion issues and XChaCha20 SecureBytes slice handling. Enhanced debug logging for version-aware salt derivation decisions. Full test suite: 1535 tests passing, 0 failures. Format Version 9 security model validated across all KDF algorithms (PBKDF2, Argon2, Scrypt, Balloon).",
    "1.4.0b10": "INDEPENDENT XOR & PARALLEL PROCESSING (BETA): Implemented Format Version 11 with Massey's Independent XOR composition providing 'strongest component' security guarantee where key remains secure even if all algorithms except the strongest are broken. Added optional parallel KDF processing with ~2.7x performance improvement (8.5s → 3.1s with 8 algorithms on 8-core CPU) using ProcessPoolExecutor for true CPU parallelism. New CLI flags: --independent-xor enables v11 format, --parallel-kdf enables parallel processing (requires --independent-xor), --kdf-workers N specifies worker count (default: auto-detect). Added comprehensive parallel_kdf.py module (739 lines) with Queue-based progress aggregation and GUI integration. Enhanced decrypt path with format version 11 support in 5 key locations. Added metadata_v11_schema.json with xor_mode field to distinguish Independent XOR (v11) from Sequential XOR (v10). Includes 7 new v11 Independent XOR tests and 11 parallel KDF tests. Full test suite: 1573 tests passing (up from 1535). Cross-branch compatible with v9 format in 1.3.x branch for seamless migration.",
    "1.4.2": "DESKTOP GUI & CRYPTO DEFAULTS: Added Simple/Pro mode toggle for desktop GUI — Simple mode (default) hides all advanced crypto options and uses the STANDARD template automatically. Modernized STANDARD template: hashes updated to SHA3-512 + BLAKE3 (10k rounds each), KDFs changed to RandomX (10 rounds) + Argon2 (10 rounds), encryption upgraded to 2-layer cascade (AES-256-GCM + ChaCha20-Poly1305). Independent XOR (v11) now enabled by default for STANDARD and PARANOID templates. Added RandomX support to independent XOR key derivation path. Added progress bars for multi-round Argon2 and RandomX in XOR mode. Dependency bumps: cryptography 46.0.6, requests 2.33.0, black 26.3.1, nltk 3.9.4.",
    "1.4.3": "BUGFIX: Fixed Flutter GUI launcher in Flatpak — corrected binary name in wrapper script so --gui flag works correctly. Fixed GTK window title from 'openssl_encrypt_mobile' to 'OpenSSL Encrypt'.",
    "1.5.0": "SECURITY HARDENING, NEW TOOLING & CODE-SURFACE REDUCTION: Major release combining a security-review fix batch, new CLI capabilities and a ~28,000-line cleanup (-23% of production code). SECURITY: authenticated v2 keystore format with HMAC-SHA256 over the whole structure, failing closed on tampering, legacy keystores auto-upgrade on save (H4/M6); PQC algorithm resolution fails closed instead of silently downgrading to the weakest available KEM (H3); portable USB drives use a unique random per-drive KDF salt instead of a global fixed salt (H5); plugin sandbox enforces file/network/process restrictions on the default process-isolation path, closes the AST frame/traceback escape and rejects world-writable plugin locations (H8); Balloon KDF gets a persisted memory-hard default space_cost on the v11 path (M3); weak 10k-iteration PBKDF2 dual-encryption verifier no longer written to metadata (M7); identity layer gains TOFU key-change detection with IdentityKeyChangedError and full-fingerprint lookup (M8); secure_memzero reports honestly on immutable input and the KDF password wipe is effective and copy-free (M10/M10a); metadata schema validation registers schemas dynamically and fails closed on unknown format_version, activating the previously unregistered v9/v12 schemas (M11). Fixed a cascade + streaming per-chunk nonce reuse: streaming a cascade chain reused one cascade salt for every chunk, and since each cascade layer derives its key and AEAD nonce from (master_key, salt) this reused per-layer nonces across all chunks (catastrophic AEAD nonce reuse for multi-chunk cascade v12 files); each chunk now derives a unique cascade salt via HKDF (metadata streaming.cascade_nonce_scheme=2), legacy files are read as scheme 1 for backward compatibility and emit a security warning urging rekey, and non-cascade streaming was unaffected. FEATURES: real 192-bit XChaCha20-Poly1305 nonces per draft-irtf-cfrg-xchacha-03 (new modules/xchacha.py, metadata flag xchacha_nonce_format=2, auto-detected legacy decryption); 'info' action reconstructs the full encrypt CLI from file metadata including removed-algorithm migration hints; Diceware passphrase generation for generate-password (--dice, bundled EFF large wordlist, entropy reporting); derive-password gains HSM-aware deterministic derivation (--hsm/--hsm-slot) and --confirm; OnlyKey Challenge-Response HSM plugin with identity protection and cross-device deterministic pepper guarantee. FIXED: streaming data-loss bug where streaming files recorded the wrong format_version; pytest collection regression that silently skipped 91 tests in unittests.py since 2025-12-30. REMOVED (code-surface reduction): steganography subsystem (numpy dependency dropped; extract hidden data with v1.4.x before upgrading), D-Bus service/client, in-package security testing framework, advisory configuration tooling (config-wizard, analyze-config, analyze-security, template subcommand, smart-recommendations) and dead security_report.py. Encryption templates (-t/--template, --quick/--standard/--paranoid, custom JSON/YAML) work unchanged; decryption cost estimator retained as DoS protection; all plugins retained. Full backward compatibility: every decrypt path preserved - all files encrypted with earlier versions still decrypt. Test suite green through five staged removals (4602 tests passing, zero regressions). CROSS-DEVICE HSM: yubikey_hsm/onlykey_hsm now form a protocol-compatible family - files encrypted with one device decrypt with the other via explicit --hsm when both hold the same HMAC-SHA1 secret; --hsm-slot on decrypt/rekey overrides the metadata slot (previously printed but silently ignored). OnlyKey plugin fixed for real hardware (PID masquerade as OTP-only YubiKey, OtpConnection request, actionable challenge-rejection errors) and new ONLYKEY_SETUP.md guide (udev rules, unlock-then-touch flow, challenge-code caveat). threefish_native builds on Python 3.14 (PyO3 0.26). Declared Python floor raised to >=3.11, aligning metadata with the documented requirement and the 1.4.x line; 3.14 classifiers added. ASCII ARMOR: new encrypt --armor/-a flag writes PEM-style Base64 (ASCII-armored) output that is safe to paste into email, chat, YAML or a clipboard; the encrypted blob is wrapped in a BEGIN/END OPENSSL-ENCRYPT MESSAGE envelope with an OpenPGP-style CRC-24 checksum that detects paste truncation (fails closed on malformed armor). decrypt/info/verify auto-detect armored input by content (no flag needed) and the round-trip is byte-exact; works across the symmetric, stdin->stdout streaming and recipient/asymmetric output paths. New module modules/armor.py, covered by the signed integrity manifest. ENCRYPT-TO-SELF: when encrypting for recipients (--for-identity), the sender's own identity (--sign-with) is now added as an additional recipient by default so the sender can later decrypt their own outbound file (removing a common data-loss footgun); the sender slot reuses the existing multi-recipient ML-KEM wrapping and is de-duplicated by fingerprint. Opt out with --no-encrypt-to-self. DETACHED FILE SIGNING: new sign/verify-signature actions produce and verify a detached post-quantum (ML-DSA-65) signature over an arbitrary file, closing the authenticity gap of symmetric AEAD (where anyone who knows the password can forge a valid file). sign --input F --sign-with IDENTITY writes a .sig JSON sidecar (ASCII-armored by default, --no-armor for raw) binding the file's SHA-512 to the signer over a domain-separated payload distinct from the encrypted-file metadata signature. verify-signature resolves the signer's public key from the local identity store and contacts by the embedded fingerprint, fails closed on an unknown signer (--signer to pin), reports which component(s) verified, and supports --json. The sidecar signatures list is forward-compatible with a future classical hybrid component. New module modules/file_signature.py, covered by the signed integrity manifest. FOREIGN-FORMAT INTEROP (decrypt --from age): read-only decryption of age/rage files to ease migration. Supports X25519 recipients (--age-identity keys.txt) and scrypt passphrase files (age -p via --password), binary and ASCII-armored input, and skips unknown/GREASE stanzas. Implemented directly on pyca cryptography (no new dependency) and validated against the age reference implementation's known-answer vectors. Untrusted-input hardening: header MAC and every payload chunk authenticated before any plaintext is returned, scrypt must be the only stanza, work factor capped, bounded reads. New package modules/interop/. Also adds OpenPGP symmetric decryption (decrypt --from pgp): passphrase-based gpg -c messages parsed directly on pyca (no GnuPG dependency) - SKESK + S2K, SEIPD v1 (CFB + SHA-1 MDC), inner ZIP/ZLIB/BZIP2 compression, ciphers 3DES/CAST5/AES/Camellia, validated against real GnuPG output. The unauthenticated SED packet is refused and decompression/packet sizes are bounded. OpenPGP public-key messages are also supported (decrypt --from pgp --pgp-key FILE): parses an exported secret key (unlocked with the key passphrase) and unwraps the session key for RSA (PKCS#1 v1.5) and ECDH (RFC 6637 KDF + RFC 3394 key-wrap) over Curve25519 and NIST P-256/384/521, validated against real GnuPG keypairs. ENVELOPE ENCRYPTION (DEK/KEK): new opt-in --envelope mode encrypts bulk data under a random Data Encryption Key (DEK) and wraps that DEK with a Key Encryption Key derived from the password through the full unchanged KDF chain (stored as encryption.wrapped_dek; decryption auto-detects). Enables O(header) credential rekey - changing the password rewraps the small DEK and rewrites only the metadata header, retaining the bulk ciphertext verbatim instead of re-encrypting it (this rotates the access credential, not the data key; full re-encrypt remains available for true data-key rotation). The bulk AEAD binds a canonical stable subset of the metadata that excludes only the KEK-gating fields a rekey changes (KDF salt/config + wrapped_dek), so a rekey keeps the ciphertext valid while tampering any authenticated field still fails closed. For a cascade chain the DEK is wrapped under the SAME chain (not single AES-256-GCM) so the envelope is never weaker than the payload. Opt-in and fully backward-compatible: non-envelope files are byte-for-byte unchanged and keep full-metadata binding; DEK/KEK are zeroed on every path and never logged. Foundation for future multi-password/multi-recipient wrapping. Fixed a pre-release cross-version envelope bug: a 1.4.x file using --envelope with a cascade chain containing xchacha20-poly1305 could not be decrypted after upgrading to 1.5.x - 1.4.x wraps the DEK with the legacy 12-byte XChaCha nonce and writes no xchacha_nonce_format flag, but the envelope DEK-unwrap hardcoded the new 192-bit format (xchacha_nonce_format=2), so the DEK never authenticated and the file was permanently unreadable (the break was unique to envelope+xchacha; the bulk cascade path already defaulted an absent flag to legacy 1, so plain cascade+xchacha and single xchacha always interoperated); the DEK wrap/unwrap now honor the file's xchacha_nonce_format (absent => legacy 1) on both the decrypt and rekey paths, newly written 1.5.x files are unchanged and still use the real 192-bit construction, and a committed genuine 1.4.x vector guards the path. SECURITY REVIEW FOLLOW-UP (2026-07-07, each fixed with a regression test and crypto-reviewer sign-off): H1 [HSM-1] the hsm fido2-test command printed the full 32-byte derived hardware pepper as hex to stdout unconditionally, outside the debug_secret() redaction chokepoint — the hex dump is removed (length still reported). M1 [PLUGIN-2] the plugin loader verified a signature over one read while AST-scanning and executing others (verify-A/execute-B) and could exec a cached .pyc never covered by the signature — _validate_plugin_file now reads the plugin once as raw bytes threaded through the signature gate, the sha256(raw) pin and ast.parse(raw), and load_plugin re-reads once, compares to the pin, and executes via compile(raw)+exec instead of spec.loader.exec_module, killing the CRLF/BOM and .pyc-shadow discrepancies (fail-closed on a missing pin for non-built-ins). H2 [PLUGIN-1] a package plugin signed only its __init__.py while transitively importing unverified sibling modules (a signed __init__.py plus a malicious unsigned helper.py executed unchecked) — a signed per-package PLUGIN.manifest with one detached PLUGIN.manifest.asc now covers every importable module (source .py, bytecode .pyc, native .so/.pyd, recursively including underscore-prefixed and nested), verified via the existing trust anchors plus an exact on-disk tree match with every module hash-pinned and every source sibling AST-scanned; under enforce a tampered/unlisted/native-swapped/impostor-signed sibling is refused, warn warns and loads, built-in packages keep the trust shortcut; plugin sign auto-detects a package and writes the manifest (a runtime import-hook for the validation-to-import TOCTOU window remains a documented follow-on). M2 [MEM-1] several callers wrapped an immutable bytes secret in bytearray(...) and called secure_memzero on the throwaway copy (a no-op wipe) in parallel_kdf.py, asymmetric_core.py, pqc.py and crypt_core.py — each secret is now held in a bytearray/SecureBytes from creation and that object is wiped. M3 [KDF-1] the parallel and sequential Independent-XOR paths derived different keys for Argon2 rounds greater than 1 — the parallel worker now matches the sequential derivation exactly. R1 [KDF-2b] a RandomX failure in the parallel Independent-XOR path was swallowed (fail-open) unlike the sequential path — both now fail closed. M4 [STREAM-1] a streaming per-chunk AEAD authentication failure was classified by substring-matching the exception text — classification is now structural. LEGACY KEM DECRYPT RETRY: PQC files written by 1.4.x releases up to 1.4.7 used a bare-SHA256 KEM key at format versions 12/13 and could not be decrypted on this line; an authenticated one-shot legacy retry now reads them (the AEAD tag rejects wrong keys, so no downgrade surface) with a re-encryption notice. EMBEDDED PQC KEY FIX: v11-v13 files with an embedded password-encrypted post-quantum private key always failed to decrypt (Missing PQC key salt) because the salt lookup was gated on format versions 4-10 — the gate now covers every v4+ format, with regression round-trips. FORMAT VERSION 14 GROUNDWORK (not yet the default): new independent-XOR-only format version 14 with fail-closed guards (no v14 sequential-XOR file can exist, unknown future versions are refused even without the schema validator). V14 TLV KDF SEED: format version 14 seeds the independent-XOR derivation from length-prefixed password/salt/pepper fields instead of raw concatenation (finding #100 boundary ambiguity), pinned for cross-line byte-identity; everything below v14 derives byte-identically. V14 KEM TRANSCRIPT BINDING: v14 PQC files bind the KEM symmetric key to the full transcript (algorithm, AEAD choice and a digest of the KEM encapsulation ciphertext) via HKDF-SHA256, with the legacy decrypt retry scoped to v12/v13 only. FORMAT VERSION 14 IS NOW THE DEFAULT WRITE FORMAT: new encryptions (including streaming) default to the independent-XOR robust-combiner topology, wide keys (AES-SIV, Threefish) derive at full length on every v14 path, sequential XOR remains a supported opt-in pinned at format version 13, explicit version requests are honored, and every existing file decrypts unchanged. STREAMING + SEQUENTIAL-XOR DATA-LOSS FIX: that combination silently wrote files that always failed chunk authentication on decrypt; it is now refused with a clear error pointing to --no-streaming. KEYSTORE VERSION-GATE FIX: keystore dual-encryption metadata lookups were gated on format versions 4-10, failing for v11+ files — the gates now cover every v4+ format. RANDOMX WIDE-KEY FIX: RandomX-enabled configs crashed key derivation for AES-SIV/Threefish (the 32-byte RandomX output is now HKDF-normalized to the full key length; 32-byte-key files are unaffected). REMOVED-PBKDF2 MIGRATION ERROR: decrypting a 1.4.x sequential file that used the removed PBKDF2 chain stage now fails up front with guidance naming the breaking change and the migration path (decrypt with 1.4.x, re-encrypt) instead of a generic authentication error; independent-XOR files carrying the same metadata entry keep decrypting. V14 REVIEW REMEDIATIONS (crypto-reviewer pass 2026-07-10, two Low findings): the v14 TLV KDF seed is hashed through a memoryview instead of an immutable bytes copy, so no unwipeable copy of the cleartext password/salt/pepper is materialized (M2 [MEM-1] hygiene; derived keys byte-identical per the cross-line golden vectors), and the KEM transcript-binding documentation now states that ciphertext/metadata substitution is detected via AEAD authentication (wrong key fails the tag), so the PQC symmetric layer must remain an AEAD (all reachable PQC data ciphers are AEADs, including Threefish via CTR+Poly1305). V14 SEED ENCODER SINGLE-ALLOCATION (post-v14 review LOW-1, 2026-07-11): the v14 TLV KDF seed was built with incremental bytearray growth, so CPython reallocations could leave partial copies of the length-prefixed cleartext password in freed unwiped heap (the final secure_memzero wipes only the last allocation) and the bytes() field conversion materialized an unwipeable copy of mutable secrets — the seed is now written into one exact-size preallocated bytearray through memoryviews (no reallocation, no immutable copies; M2 [MEM-1] hygiene); not exploitable on its own, derived keys and the on-disk format are byte-identical (cross-line golden vectors plus a new byte-identity test matrix). KEYSTORE VERSION-GATE TYPE SAFETY (post-v14 review LOW-2, 2026-07-11): the keystore integration read format_version from raw parsed JSON and compared it with >=, so a crafted file carrying a non-int value crashed the operation with an unhandled TypeError (fail-closed either way — no bypass, key material never touched, but a crash is a one-operation DoS) — all six affected ingestion points in keystore_wrapper/keystore_utils now validate the type once via a shared helper and reject malformed metadata with a clean ValidationError; legitimately written files always store an int, so behavior for every valid file is unchanged. RECIPIENT PASSWORD-WRAP TRANSCRIPT BINDING (post-v14 review LOW-3, 2026-07-11): the per-recipient AES-256-GCM wrap key was derived from the ML-KEM shared secret with a static HKDF info (the finding-#83 transcript binding covered only the main PQC data path) — new asymmetric files now bind the KEM encapsulation ciphertext (SHA-256 digest) and the KEM algorithm identity into the wrap-key derivation and record wrap_version 3 in the signed recipient entry; defense-in-depth (ML-KEM implicit rejection plus the GCM tag already defeat substitution in practice), fail-closed by construction (a marked entry never falls back to weaker derivations, a stripped marker derives the wrong key and fails the tag, unknown markers are rejected); every existing file keeps decrypting via the unchanged v2->v1 chain (pinned by a pre-fix fixture), while asymmetric files written from this version on cannot be opened by older releases (clean unwrap error — same trade as the v14 default flip). REMOTE/COMBINED PEPPER ZEROIZATION (v14 follow-up review LOW-1, gitlab#113, 2026-07-11): the remote pepper (AES-GCM decrypt output) and the combined hsm+remote pepper concatenation were immutable bytes that outlived the carefully wiped v14 TLV KDF seed, and the hsm_pepper wipe was a no-op copy-zero on plugin-supplied bytes (M10) — all three are now held in wipeable bytearrays from creation (the combined pepper built by a new _combine_peppers helper in one exact-size allocation, M2 [MEM-1] standard) and zeroized in the encrypt/decrypt finally blocks on all paths; unavoidable immutable library/plugin transients remain documented accepted residuals; no derived keys or file formats change. FUTURE FORMAT-VERSION WRITE BOUND (v14 follow-up review LOW-2, gitlab#114, 2026-07-11): encrypt_file accepted an explicit format_version above the latest stable format and wrote a file the decrypt side fails closed on (permanently unreadable — data-loss footgun for API callers, not attacker-exploitable); new writes now raise a clear ValueError for format_version > LATEST_STABLE_FORMAT_VERSION before any output is produced, mirroring the decrypt-side bound; no change for any valid version. NATIVE THREEFISH KEY ZEROIZATION (v14 follow-up review LOW-3, gitlab#115, 2026-07-11): the native PQC Threefish encrypt/decrypt branches (and the adapter encrypt branch) derived the actual 64/128-byte Threefish data-encryption key via HKDF as plain immutable bytes and never wiped it — all three sites now hold the expanded key in SecureBytes and zeroize it in a finally on success and exception paths, mirroring the adapter decrypt pattern; no derived keys or ciphertexts change. LEGACY-KDF RETRY CLASSIFICATION (v14 follow-up review LOW-4, gitlab#116, 2026-07-11): three fail-closed classification gaps fixed — native Threefish tag failures are now classified structurally (RuntimeError from the native binding -> PQCAuthenticationError) so legacy bare-SHA256-keyed v12/v13 threefish files reach the one-shot legacy retry; the adapter retry now also catches the project AuthenticationError raised by the custom XChaCha wrapper (adapter xchacha legacy files previously skipped the retry); and the native classifier is narrowed from except-Exception to (InvalidTag, AuthenticationError) so structural errors propagate instead of triggering a pointless retry; the retry stays scoped to v12/v13 and AEAD-gated, v14+ never retries. HSM TEST-COMMAND PEPPER PRINT — LIVE-PATH RESIDUAL (H1 [HSM-1] residual, gitlab#121, 2026-07-12, ported from 1.4.x): the earlier H1 fix removed the pepper hex dump from modules/hsm_cli.py, a click frontend that is not wired into the openssl-encrypt entry point — the live hsm fido2-test/onlykey-test handlers in crypt_cli.py still printed the full derived hardware pepper as hex; both now report only the length (mitigating: the printed pepper came from a random per-invocation test salt, so it exposed no real file, but it is hardware-derived key material); additionally the FIDO2 pepper plugin no longer interpolates raw prf/hmac-secret output (the pepper's source material) into debug logs or PluginResult error messages that reach the user via eprint (structure only: type/keys), and the asymmetric_core __main__ self-test prints lengths instead of its (random, throwaway) roundtrip password hex on failure; the H1 regression test now scans the live crypt_cli.py, the OnlyKey/YubiKey challenge-response plugins and every prf_data sink, not just hsm_cli.py. DERIVE-PASSWORD MEMORY HYGIENE (security review 2026-07-13 LOW-1, gitlab#123): the derive-password handler held the HSM pepper as unwipeable immutable bytes and wiped only a throwaway copy of the derived key — the pepper is now held in a wipeable buffer from acquisition (a mutable plugin buffer is wiped in place) and the truncated output buffer is a bytearray, both zeroized in a finally on all exit paths; the immutable generate_key return remains a documented accepted residual (M10); derived outputs unchanged. LEGACY SEED ZEROIZATION (security review 2026-07-13 INFO-1, gitlab#124): the no-hash-iterations branch of generate_key built its KDF seed as an immutable password+salt+pepper concatenation the return-site wipe silently refused (M10) — the seed is now written into one exact-size wipeable bytearray through a memoryview and effectively zeroized in the finally, including when a KDF rebinds the working variable; legacy sequential formats only, derived keys byte-identical (golden-value regression tests). BALLOON V14 FAIL-CLOSED (security review 2026-07-13 INFO-2, gitlab#125): the decrypt-side balloon derivation fell back to the weak space_cost=16 when the field was absent from metadata — kept for v11-13 where it is load-bearing (pre-M3 v1.4.0-v1.4.3 files really derived with 16), but refused with a clear ValueError for format_version >= 14, where every released writer persists the field and a missing value can only be crafted/corrupted metadata; the parallel path needs no gate (v13+ parallel dispatch delegates to the sequential gated path, invariant pinned by a test); no legitimately written file changes behavior. KDF COST MEMORY CEILING (gitlab#128, GHSA-7894-5gw8-69hr): Argon2 memory_cost, scrypt N and balloon space_cost read from untrusted file metadata or a keystore header were fed to the KDF before authentication with no upper bound (scrypt's own maxmem was even derived from the attacker's N, so it could never trip), letting a single crafted file drive a multi-terabyte allocation and OOM-crash the host on decrypt with no correct password — decryption now estimates peak memory before any KDF runs and refuses above an 8 GiB safety ceiling (4x the largest shipped preset, so no legitimate file is affected), overridable per file with --allow-high-kdf-cost or an interactive confirmation; the same ceiling guards standard/streaming decrypt, the envelope rekey fast-path, recovery-slot add/remove, asymmetric --no-verify decrypt and the PQC keystore header, and the scrypt memory estimate (previously under-reported as zero) is corrected; CPU/time cost stays advisory since only memory OOM-kills uninterruptibly. IDENTITY-FILE KDF COST BOUND (gitlab#129, GHSA-783h-8q2f-f762): an identity file's password-protection block carried an unbounded Argon2 memory_cost read straight from JSON that _derive_key fed to hash_secret_raw before the AEAD tag authenticated the private key, so a tampered/attacker-authored identity with a gigabyte-scale memory_cost OOM-crashed the host on unlock pre-authentication (same class as gitlab#128 on the identity-file surface) — the Argon2 cost parameters are now clamped to sane maxima (memory <= 2 GiB, time <= 64, parallelism <= 16) before derivation; legitimate identities use the 64 MB default, far under the cap, so no file changes behavior. PLUGIN SIGNATURE ENFORCE-BY-DEFAULT (gitlab#130, GHSA-587j-4r3v-cm2c): the plugin loader defaulted to the warn signature policy, so an unsigned/unverifiable non-built-in plugin was exec'd in the host process after only a bypassable AST denylist scan (getattr/__import__ indirection), giving arbitrary code execution to anyone able to drop a .py into a plugin directory — the default policy is now enforce: a non-built-in plugin must carry a valid detached signature from an enrolled trust anchor or it is refused before its code is imported; built-in bundled plugins keep their trust shortcut and are unaffected, so no shipped functionality changes; users who deliberately load unsigned third-party plugins can opt back in with OPENSSL_ENCRYPT_PLUGIN_SIGNATURE_POLICY=warn (or off) or an explicit signature_policy= argument, and an unrecognized env value now fails closed to enforce with a warning instead of silently weakening the policy; the built-in trust shortcut is now scoped to the genuinely shipped plugin subtree so the advertised third-party drop directories (plugins/user, plugins/community, plugins/official) are no longer treated as built-in and must pass the full signature+AST+TOCTOU gate (otherwise the ENFORCE default would have been bypassable by dropping an unsigned plugin there). PQC KEYFILE ARGON2ID WRAP (gitlab#131 F16, GHSA-fmjx-p826-6fvr): a .pqc keyfile created with --pqc-keyfile wrapped the long-lived PQC private key under a PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 key at only 100,000 iterations (below the OWASP floor, far weaker than the Argon2id used for file/keystore material), so an attacker who obtained the keyfile could brute-force the wrapping password cheaply offline — new keyfiles now derive the wrapping key with Argon2id and record a self-describing key_kdf descriptor (redundant trailing SHA-256 dropped); existing PBKDF2-wrapped keyfiles still decrypt unchanged via the legacy no-key_kdf path, and the Argon2 cost read from a keyfile is bounded (memory <= 2 GiB, time <= 64, parallelism <= 16) so a tampered keyfile cannot OOM the host on decrypt before the AES-GCM tag authenticates; re-wrap existing keyfiles to move them onto Argon2id. DUAL-ENCRYPTION WEAK VERIFIER REMOVED (gitlab#131 F18, GHSA-fmjx-p826-6fvr): dual-encrypted files carried a pqc_dual_encrypt_verify hash (a 10k-iteration PBKDF2 pre-check over the file password stored in cleartext metadata) that an attacker with the file could brute-force offline to recover the second-factor file password — that pre-check is no longer recomputed, trusted, or propagated into re-processed metadata; the file password is authenticated by the dual-encryption AES-GCM tag during keystore key retrieval (keystore Argon2id KDF, not brute-forceable offline), and the one state where that tag would not gate the file password (a file claiming dual encryption backed by a non-dual keystore entry — a metadata/keystore mismatch) now fails closed in get_key; legacy dual-encrypted files still decrypt unchanged, re-encrypt them to drop the weak verifier from metadata. PORTABLE-USB INTEGRITY + MANIFEST SALT (gitlab#132 F13/F19, GHSA-8jx3-27qf-3p97): the portable-USB threat model treats the removable drive as untrusted, but integrity verification only re-hashed the files listed in the manifest — a file ADDED to the drive, including a root-level autorun.* payload (auto-executed by the OS on insert), was never detected and verification still passed; new drives now write a v2 integrity manifest that is an allowlist of every file in the tool tree (plus the root autorun hashes), so verification flags ANY file added afterward — a planted .dll/.so/.pyd/.exe or any other payload, not just a fixed extension set — and any tampered/added/removed root autorun file (the user's mutable workspace data/ and logs/ are excluded, so normal use still verifies); drives created before the fix carry no v2 marker and verify exactly as before, re-create a drive to gain the stronger checks; additionally the cryptographic hash-manifest fallback path now derives its key with the drive's unique per-drive salt instead of the global fixed salt (F19). LOW SECURITY BATCH (gitlab#133-136): PLUGIN SANDBOX PATH PREFIX (F15, GHSA-vr4h-5xqv-xxxf) — PluginSandbox._is_safe_path authorized paths with a bare string prefix, so a sandboxed plugin foo could access a sibling .../plugins/foobar; each allowed directory is now matched as itself or with a trailing separator, refusing prefix-sharing siblings. KEYSERVER TOKEN DEBUG REDACTION (F17, GHSA-jqqp-pf9j-889j) — the 'keyserver set-token <token>' positional bearer token was echoed cleartext in the --debug argv dump; it is now routed through the debug_secret redaction chokepoint (rotate any token previously used with --debug). TRUST-ANCHOR FULL-FINGERPRINT (F21, GHSA-xg52-638v-jc5m) — enroll_trust_key accepted a suffix/partial fingerprint match, so a forgeable short key id could enrol an attacker's colliding key as a trusted plugin-signing anchor; enrollment now requires the exact full fingerprint. PIV PIN MEMORY HYGIENE (F20) — the PIN is now decoded from its wiped bytearray directly instead of through an extra immutable bytes() copy, materializing one fewer unwipeable copy (the binding-required str is an accepted minimized-lifetime residual); no behavior change. IDENTITY DISPLAY ESCAPE SANITIZATION (gitlab#172, GHSA-qjr2-x6mr-8xgf): Identity.import_public validated the identity name but took email completely raw, and the CLI printed it unsanitized directly above the Fingerprint: line in identity import/list/show - a JSON string may carry unicode-escaped control characters (the JSON security validator rejects only LITERAL control characters, whose escaped source text is printable), so a crafted bundle's email could carry ANSI cursor-movement sequences that overwrite the genuine fingerprint line with one the victim trusts, attacking the out-of-band fingerprint comparison that is the identity design's only authenticity mechanism; the same class existed on error paths, where a rejected identity name or --alias was interpolated verbatim into the IdentityError message the CLI echoes; security review of the fix found the same class live on every other identity display surface, including one reachable by a fully remote attacker: the keyserver TOFU trust prompt printed a fetched bundle's name/email/created_at raw around the very fingerprint it tells the user to verify out of band (the bundle's self-signature is no defence - it verifies against the signing key shipped IN the bundle), identity create --email was the producer-side gap (the value is exported verbatim and uploaded, attacking other users' prompts), stored identity files were a third channel (an --identity-store directory from an archive or shared folder feeds list/show and the TOFU key-change warning without any import-time check), a signature sidecar leaked through three fields (signer_fingerprint printed on the unknown-signer error path before any cryptographic check, algorithm echoed by the unsupported-algorithm error pre-verification, and the display-only component name - deliberately excluded from the signed payload, so any tamperer can rewrite it on a VALID signature - printed inside the GOOD-signature verdict block), and a keyserver's raw HTTP error body (unbounded remote text) reached the terminal through exception messages; the fix validates at every boundary and sanitizes at every display: import_public, Identity.generate, Identity.load and PublicKeyBundle now validate email (string, at most 320 chars, no control characters), fingerprint (the colon-separated lowercase hex shape the tool has always written) and created_at (at most 64 chars, no controls), parse_signature format-validates signer_fingerprint, algorithm and every component name, keyserver HTTP error bodies are truncated and sanitized, identity.json is read bounded/UTF-8/through the JSON security validator so a hostile store directory cannot DoS identity list, and a display sanitizer escapes C0 controls, DEL, the C1 range (one-byte CSI introducers included), backslash (so escaped output is unambiguous) and the bidi/format controls (honoured by VTE/Kitty, able to visually reverse an email within its line) at every terminal display of identity name/email/fingerprint, the keyserver trust prompt and search display, the TOFU key-change warning, the verified-signature sender line and every identity CLI error path - escaping rather than stripping keeps the evidence visible, and contacts and cached keyserver bundles imported by earlier versions with crafted fields are neutralized at display time by the same sanitizer. GUI IDENTITY LISTING (gitlab#183, github#100): the desktop GUI ran 'identity list --include-contacts --json', but identity list declared no --json flag, so argparse exited 2 and the GUI swallowed the failure into empty lists - the Identity Management screen, the Decrypt tab's identity selector and the main window all showed an empty identity list with no error, so GUI identity listing had never worked (third emitted-but-nonexistent flag after gitlab#164 and gitlab#181); identity list --json now emits a single JSON document on stdout (own/contacts lists of name, email, fingerprint, kem_algorithm, sig_algorithm, created_at) and nothing else, since the GUI feeds all of stdout to a JSON parser, with the human report unchanged without the flag; the JSON channel is deliberately NOT display-sanitized (machine-readable, the transport escapes control characters, the consumer renders), so display safety belongs to the renderer - the GUI sanitizes at the decode boundary rather than per widget (a per-widget pass had already missed the recipient picker and the signature-verification picker, the two controls deciding who can read the plaintext and whose signature is trusted), its sanitizer now escapes rather than blanks (matching the CLI's escape-not-strip rule) and covers the C1 range, the bidi/format controls, U+2028/U+2029 and the zero-width set while leaving ZWNJ/ZWJ untouched as load-bearing in Persian/Indic scripts and emoji sequences, and since Flutter honours bidi overrides and treats U+2028/U+2029 as mandatory line breaks those characters are now rejected at the CLI import boundary too, alongside gitlab#172's terminal-control class; two failure modes that let this bug hide are closed with it - the GUI no longer turns a failed identity list into an empty identity list (it raises, so the screen's previously-dead error banner renders) and a store entry that fails to load is reported rather than silently absent in both the JSON (skipped key) and the human output, since presenting a short list as complete would silently drop a recipient or make an own identity look deleted. IDENTITY NAMESPACE COLLISION (gitlab#173, GHSA-8gmx-w9m8-vx7q): get_by_name resolves own identities before contacts, but add_identity chose its destination purely from whether the identity was an own one, so importing a contact whose name matched an existing own identity created a shadowed contact entry - invisible while the own identity existed, since every lookup resolved the own identity first - and delete_identity removed only the first location it found, so deleting the own identity left the shadow behind and get_by_name then resolved to the contact's keys under a name the user trusts; recipient resolution goes through get_by_name, making this a live key substitution where files encrypted to that name go to the attacker's key, and the TOFU key-change dialogue is no defence because it gates a CHANGED KEY (passable with --allow-key-change, and silent when the fingerprints match); add_identity now refuses a name that already exists as the other kind in both directions and independently of the key-change gate, delete_identity removes both locations so a pre-existing shadow cannot be promoted, and IdentityStore.find_shadowed_names() plus identity list (human output and the --json shadowed key) surface a collision that is otherwise invisible. Review of the fix caught that deleting both sides had become a trap - the warning told users to remove the entry they did not intend to keep, but identity delete had no way to do that and removed both, destroying the own identity's private keys (making every file encrypted to it unreadable) and the contact's TOFU pin, so a later import of that name would be accepted as first use with no key-change warning; identity delete now takes --kind own|contact|both (default both), shows both entries and both fingerprints before the confirmation and states what each deletion costs, and 'contacts' is reserved as an entry name because it is the store's own container directory, so an entry of that name was written INTO it - unlistable yet resolvable, and deleting it would have removed every pinned contact in the store. ENVELOPE REWRITE DATA LOSS (gitlab#148): _write_envelope_header had an atomic path and a truncating one, and the truncating path opened the destination in truncating write mode — a crash or ENOSPC part-way through left a shortened, unreadable file and no copy of the original, which when the destination IS the input is the user's only ciphertext, defeating the whole point of recovery slots; the writer now DERIVES whether the write lands on its own input immediately before writing rather than trusting the caller's in_place flag (a caller that omits it cannot truncate in place, one that asserts it cannot skip the exclusions), fsyncs before the rename and fchmods the descriptor rather than the path; three cases cannot use os.replace because it installs a new inode — a symlink on either side (the link itself would be replaced), a file with more than one hard link (the other name would keep the old envelope, for remove-recovery a silent revocation failure reported as success) and anything that is not a regular file — and those are written THROUGH the existing inode after copying the original to an fsynced backup beside it, restored if the write fails and kept (with its path in the error) if the restore fails too; the envelope rekey fast-path carried a second, weaker copy of this logic with no fsync and no same-file check, so the guarantee reached the recovery-slot commands only, and now delegates to the same writer. PQC KEYFILE CLEARTEXT DUPLICATE + UNREACHABLE SAVE (gitlab#157): crypt_cli.py carried two independent copies of the --pqc-keyfile save/load logic -- 320305ee added password-wrapping to the copy that existed then, c41a3a1cb reconstructed the file and reintroduced the pre-fix plaintext pattern as a second copy, and aef4ab42 (gitlab#131/F16) later upgraded the wrapping to Argon2id believing it touched the one write site; the duplicate wrote private_key as bare base64 with no key_encrypted marker and its loader read private_key unconditionally, so a properly wrapped keyfile would have had its AES-GCM ciphertext base64-decoded and used as the key -- the duplicate is deleted. Separately --pqc-keyfile could not save through the documented encrypt subcommand: the save branch is gated on --pqc-gen-key, declared only on a vestigial monolithic parser, so encrypt --pqc-gen-key exited 2 with unrecognized arguments and naming a non-existent path matched neither branch and raised nothing (an ephemeral key, no file, and no way to open the ciphertext later); the legacy argument ordering with an option before the subcommand did reach the monolithic parser and save, which is how the cleartext writer was reachable at all. --pqc-gen-key is now declared on the encrypt subparser, a non-existent keyfile without it is refused with an instruction, --pqc-gen-key without --pqc-keyfile is refused too, and the keyfile is resolved once before the overwrite branch so both output paths use the same keypair -- deleting the duplicate without moving that left --overwrite encrypting with an ephemeral key and reporting a bad keyfile only after the input had been replaced. The keyfile is written through create_secure_file(exclusive=True) so it is created 0600 instead of at the umask, a pre-planted symlink or FIFO at that path is refused rather than followed, and an existing file is never silently clobbered. On the 1.5.x line the bug was live on every same-file recovery-slot rewrite: recovery_slots.py called add_recovery_slots/remove_recovery_slot with no in_place argument at all, so -o equal to -i always took the truncating path, and this line's writer additionally lacked the fsync and chmod'd the path after the rename instead of fchmod'ing the descriptor. PLUGIN PEPPER MANAGEMENT CLI (gitlab#193): the remote-pepper plugin's operations had working business logic but no argparse surface, so the desktop GUI's pepper controls emitted argv (plugin pepper list/test/setup-totp/verify-totp/configure-deadman) that exited 2 with 'invalid choice' and the GUI swallowed the failure — a new `plugin pepper` subcommand tree wires all of them: list (stored peppers as JSON), test (ad-hoc mTLS connectivity probe from --url/--client-cert/--client-key/--ca-cert), setup-totp/verify-totp (TOTP 2FA enrolment) and configure-deadman (the pepper dead-man's switch); the only secret payloads — the TOTP shared secret and the one-time backup codes — are emitted on stdout ONLY (never stderr, which the GUI persists to its debug log) and registered with the audit-log redactor, mirroring generate-password --json, and --code is added to the --debug argv-redaction set; configure-deadman --interval/--grace-period take whole days and are refused below 1, because the switch auto-wipes every pepper on a missed check-in so a zero/negative deadline must never be armable with an already-past auto-wipe, and the destructive panic operation is deliberately not exposed. PLUGIN INTEGRITY VERIFICATION CLI (gitlab#194): the remote file-integrity plugin (test/stats/verify) likewise had no CLI surface, so the GUI's integrity controls could not reach it — a new `plugin integrity` subcommand tree wires test (mTLS probe), stats (verification statistics as JSON) and verify --file-id --metadata-hash, whose verify uses a deliberately FOUR-outcome contract so a file the service never registered or a service that cannot be reached can NEVER be reported as a tamper alarm (the exact bug the removed GUI batch path had, collapsing all three into one false mismatch): distinct exit codes — 0 match, 1 mismatch, 3 not-found, 4 unreachable — plus a JSON outcome field; a malformed metadata hash (not 64 hex) is a usage error rather than a mislabelled unreachable, mismatch is keyed on the service's own violation-warning signal, and the not-found path stays cautious rather than falsely reassuring because the exact server response shape is a documented client-side dependency (gitlab#194). ONLYKEY IDENTITY WRONG-DEVICE BINDING (gitlab#218, ADVISORY 2026-18, Medium, CWE-440): identity create --hsm onlykey computed the correct hsm_type but used it only for a pre-flight check — Identity.generate() had no hsm_type parameter and built the protection service with the yubikey default, and the recorded hsm_config.hsm_type was never consulted for plugin selection (encrypt and decrypt both used the default too), so an OnlyKey selection was silently dropped: with only an OnlyKey present creation failed with a misleading 'no Yubikey available' error, and with both devices present the identity was silently YubiKey-bound and could never be opened with the OnlyKey; generate() now takes an hsm_type (default yubikey, threaded from identity create) and the encrypt/decrypt paths build the service from the identity's recorded hsm_config.hsm_type so an OnlyKey identity uses the OnlyKey plugin end to end, and HSM error messages name the configured device — existing YubiKey identities are unaffected (yubikey default + legacy fallback preserved); not remotely exploitable (no adversary), recorde